{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages","id":1544582,"title":"Chronicle of Ages","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-10-23T12:21:43-07:00","id":1544582,"title":"Chronicle of Ages","content":"In all my years of experience in the WoD setting, I've always liked the idea of building our own canon world through the ages. Most of my tables usually start somewhere in the Middle Ages and then advance slowly (often times faster or slower depending on the taste of my players and what I envision for a story,e.g. some players and sometimes I myself feel not inclined to actually get into the current era, like years 2000s, etc.) through the years. As expected many things end up different in the setting, like the year of formation of Camarilla and sometimes even entire clan devlopments are utterly changed, I had a story where the Tzimisce Antediluvian was &quot;destroyed&quot; for the second time during the 1300s and another clan was founded in its stead, some other times the Giovanni ended up being totally subjulgated and the Capadocian clan was still around in the 1800s. Other times, more fantastic things happened as tradition mages had some astounding victories over the Order of Reason and technology advanced much slower than in our own world.<br /> <br /> All of these examples I give in order to ask you all, have you ever had something like this in your games that made the world totally different than the &quot;normal setting&quot;? I ask this because it's to be expected that some things are going to follow a totally different path, as for example, it would be normal if some important Canon NPCs are destroyed in our setting, as it's hard to expect things to follow exactly as they did in Canon, but I think it's interesting to consider these changes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-23T13:12:16-07:00","id":1544584,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages#post1544582\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All of these examples I give in order to ask you all, have you ever had something like this in your games that made the world totally different than the &quot;normal setting&quot;? I ask this because it's to be expected that some things are going to follow a totally different path, as for example, it would be normal if some important Canon NPCs are destroyed in our setting, as it's hard to expect things to follow exactly as they did in Canon, but I think it's interesting to consider these changes.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are two main ways that the setting changes.<br /> <br /> The first being that the PCs change a historical event and the resulting timeline is altered, the PCs prevent Lincoln from being assassinated or they assisted the Confederacy, so they won the civil war and thus world history is altered because the US took a different path. This can be fun to think about, but unless you are running a very complicated historical simulation, your average ST will just run history as it was, with the PCs contributions or alterations lost to history since the public never really knows what actually happened. <br /> <br /> The second way is for the PCs to have agency in the current time. This is where Player ingenuity has a head on collision with the setting over plot holes. The very game design states that the neonates can never out do the elders, they are eternally oppressed and at best can hope to survive for another night. But the reality of the situation is that PCs have far more potential agency than most groups use because they never consider the options. So, let's look at a few examples.<br /> <br /> 1. The vampire population is canonically relatively low, meaning that once you assign 10 - 50 cainites per city, there are hundreds of cities out there which are unclaimed by vampires. So rather than playing politics, being under the thumb of the local elders, any coterie can simply travel across the country and stake a claim on an unclaimed city or town. They are now the Prince &amp; Primogen of the place, with no need to overthrow the existing power structure. Of course, they will have to fight to maintain their control over the place, but they can sire as they wish and make the rules as they wish. So why do so many coteries stick around their city again?<br /> <br /> 2. The character creation process means you are not restricted to only making vampires who were janitors, mechanics, cops, or gang members in their mortal lives. You can be a captain of industry or other business mogul from the start. If you go with Resources 5 / Influence 5 / Contacts 5, you are making Elon Musk or Jeff Besos look like underachievers, especially since in all the fluff the richest and most influential mortals can't compare to vampires in the same situations. So, what happens if a coterie is made up of kindred with those kinds of stats? Rather than getting into a turf war with the local gang or the established kindred in their city, they just buy a city sized plot of land in the middle of the desert and build a company town from the ground up. Every aspect of the city would be designed to isolate the residents and meet all vampiric needs. Once again, the coterie becomes the ruling class of vampires rather than fighting against them in the previous city.<br /> <br /> 3. Given the rise of technology in first world countries, it is becoming an increasingly problematic plot hole to explain how vampires function in those areas without breaking the masquerade by existing inside human society. So, vampires need to hide away more than they are currently portrayed as doing in the fluff. What happens when vampire coteries start building doomsday bunkers like converted missile silos? They can have a live in herd, built in hydroponics, a solar and wind farm for power, and all business is conducted over video calls which makes Presence and Dominate useless. There is relatively little reason to maintain the Lost Boys method of existing, but then about 90% of the plot hooks wouldn't work.<br /> <br /> Breaking or changing the setting happens almost automatically if you are not artificially enforcing the outcome.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-23T13:29:13-07:00","id":1544587,"title":"","content":"Because of the issue that given enough XP, even neonate NPCs can overshadow powerful elders over the course of a long chronicle, I typically prefer to have chronicles that last many decades although I have never run a game where it lasted centuries. So I always start them in an interesting historical era. The 1920s are a favorite because I already have a lot of historical sourcebooks from the Call of Cthulhu RPG that can be quickly adapted. I did have an ambition once to run a game set in France that started at the &quot;standard&quot; 1197 era of Vampire: the Dark Ages and last until the end of the Hundred Years War which would have covered the time of the Anarch Revolt, but it ended far earlier than that.<br /> <br /> My view on things is that the PCs are important, but aren't that important. They can play vital roles that may impact the setting materially, but can't fundamentally change the historical forces driving change. That does mean I would keep them away from any fundamental events like a diablerie of the antediluvians or Tyler's attack on Hardestadt. The setting will still be recognizable, but important departures can take place.<br /> <br /> For example, a Lasombra PC who joined the Camarilla would not be able to stop most of his remaining clan from eventually joining the Sabbat, but she could become an important member of a much larger Lasombra anti-tribu, perhaps one even with a seat on the Inner Circle. Likewise perfectly fine for the Cappadocians to have a much larger presence that survived the Giovanni purge, but much of that is because I never found the purge to be that credible and the game ended up revealing all these surviving Cappadocians (though called by different bloodline names) anyway. Maybe a bloodline survives that perished, or perished that officially survives. Bloodlines aren't as relevant to the game than the main clans are.<br /> <br /> Individual NPCs are totally open to being destroyed because they are there to serve a purpose in the chronicle I am running, not to fulfill some historical destiny. I don't find any canon NPCs to be so precious that I could not tolerate their destruction. If they needed to serve some special role in canonical history, some similar NPC will work just fine. To be honest, while I do like some of the signature or canonical characters, I'm just not that impressed with Vykos, Anatole, Lucita, etc. that I would feel the need to keep them alive. They'd be treated just like any other NPCs in a city sourcebook. Sure, if some NPC really tickled my fancy, I'd do what I could to keep them around and play smart, but none of the &quot;major&quot; signature characters fit that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-23T14:33:22-07:00","id":1544593,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because of the issue that given enough XP, even neonate NPCs can overshadow powerful elders over the course of a long chronicle, I typically prefer to have chronicles that last many decades although I have never run a game where it lasted centuries. </div> </div> </div>To be honest, this is not really an issue to me, as I can ST an elder's story just as easily as a neonate one, though naturally the focus tend to differ. Also, if the players gain xp, the NPCs should as well if I feel it's necessary (as if I'm not mistaken is even stated in some book, but I ofc won't remember which). Not to mention that in this type of chronicle it's very rare for PCs to survive the whole time span. My current chronicle has about 7 years, we begun in the early 1200s and are now in the 1500s, there's not a single PC from the start of the story that is still around.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My view on things is that the PCs are important, but aren't that important. They can play vital roles that may impact the setting materially, but can't fundamentally change the historical forces driving change. That does mean I would keep them away from any fundamental events like a diablerie of the antediluvians or Tyler's attack on Hardestadt. The setting will still be recognizable, but important departures can take place. </div> </div> </div>I think it's curious (not in a bad sense, don't get me wrong) that you see they shouldn' be able to alter history in any significant way, because hell I even make this kind of change myself without player's agency when I feel like the story is going someway and/or I want see a different story. I don't feel restrained at all by any of this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Likewise perfectly fine for the Cappadocians to have a much larger presence that survived the Giovanni purge, but much of that is because I never found the purge to be that credible and the game ended up revealing all these surviving Cappadocians (though called by different bloodline names) anyway. </div> </div> </div>As someone who read basically everything in the setting I've always seen it the same way, because first it would make no sense for them to be able to kill all capadocians, second the setting itself gave clear signs the clan just changed into different things (Samedi, Harbingers of Skull, among others), not to mention characters like Marianna, etc.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-23T17:12:45-07:00","id":1544608,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544593#post1544593\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think it's curious (not in a bad sense, don't get me wrong) that you see they shouldn' be able to alter history in any significant way</div> </div> </div> </div>I think the setting still needs to be recognizable because that's generally that attracted players to the game to begin with. If the setting starts becoming wacky so that it does not look meet their expectations, they begin to disengage. It's for the same reason many players abandoned Revised when things like entire clans leaving and joining sects began to happen.<br /> <br /> Players want to see how their actions affect the chronicle. On the small scale, this is totally acceptable. No problem with the NPC Prince or whatever characters being destroyed and PCs or someone else taking their place. I hate railroads and encourage agency for the PCs. However, in my experience if the foundations of the setting itself changes, it tends to not make the game fun for players. Context is often an important thing when players create their characters and how they imagine they are going to react to the setting elements. Not all players are like that of course. Some don't mind it if during the course of play major changes happen like the entire Tremere clan being destroyed, the Tzimisce joining the Camarilla, and the Ventrue anti-tribu leading the Sabbat. But at least among my gaming groups, that subverts expectations too much. For a very short, limited chronicle, that may be OK. But it tends to get old very quick.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":".  typo 10-25-2024, 02:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T09:13:37-07:00","id":1544732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544608#post1544608\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think the setting still needs to be recognizable because that's generally that attracted players to the game to begin with. If the setting starts becoming wacky so that it does not look meet their expectations, they begin to disengage. It's for the same reason many players abandoned Revised when things like entire clans leaving and joining sects began to happen.<br /> <br /> Players want to see how their actions affect the chronicle. On the small scale, this is totally acceptable. No problem with the NPC Prince or whatever characters being destroyed and PCs or someone else taking their place. I have railroads and encourage agency for the PCs. However, in my experience if the foundations of the setting itself changes, it tends to not make the game fun for players. Context is often an important thing when players create their characters and how they imagine they are going to react to the setting elements. Not all players are like that of course. Some don't mind it if during the course of play major changes happen like the entire Tremere clan being destroyed, the Tzimisce joining the Camarilla, and the Ventrue anti-tribu leading the Sabbat. But at least among my gaming groups, that subverts expectations too much. For a very short, limited chronicle, that may be OK. But it tends to get old very quick.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I understand your concern. But in my opinion if the players begin to disengage because the setting is reacting to their actions and that of the SPCs they support, then the problem is with the players. The story should, in my opinion be always fluid and open to changes as it makes the story more interesting than one where it seems nothing the players or the SPCs make change anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T10:35:33-07:00","id":1544738,"title":"","content":"I think we have to address the fundamental difference between what the players want from the setting verses the setting default as law.<br /> <br /> Using video games as an example, some Players want a Dark Souls experience where survival is the accomplishment, some Players want a fantasy game where they can be characters with different problems than they do IRL, some Players just want to play an RPG rather than going to therapy, and some are wanting to experiment with a different persona. All of which can be done quite easily with the rules and setting as written.<br /> <br /> But what does an ST do if the Players want to do something more scripted? The default situation is everyone makes a character and is dropped into the game world to interact with all the NPCs and each other. But what does the ST do when the Player already has a beginning, middle, and end planned out? In Narrative terms this could be the rags to riches storyline but achieving the wealth is only the middle game since they want to actually play around with it. Now the middle and end game can be about anything as long as it allows the PCs to make a difference.<br /> <br /> To use the Count of Monte Cristo to frame this issue, your average storyline in a game is the PCs are betrayed and sent to prison, the game is how to escape. The sort of Players I am talking about are also wanting to play out the treasure hunt, building the influence network, setup the plan for revenge and then enact it. From there then they will probably want to play out how the characters would contend with the setting now that they have all the power and influence to be proactive rather than reactive to it.<br /> <br /> This more scripted character design seems to be common if the Player is an ST. This gets more common if most if not all of your group are STs, since their minds are essentially trained to think in story arcs more than the experimental &quot;lets see what this does&quot; style of character design.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T11:42:19-07:00","id":1544741,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544732#post1544732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The story should, in my opinion be always fluid and open to changes as it makes the story more interesting than one where it seems nothing the players or the SPCs make change anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're not listening to the points I made that the setting should in fact be impacted by the player's decisions and actions. I said that in both of my previous posts. So we're in agreement. The PCs are important. But the idea that a small group of 3-6 characters have such an impact that the choices and actions of thousands or tens of thousands of other characters are unimportant is ludicrous.<br /> <br /> There is a huge difference between saying the PCs cannot make important decisions that affect the setting and that ONLY the PCs decisions are important because they can somehow cancel out the actions of all the other NPCs in the setting. In local and small scale areas where the PCs make up a significant faction of characters, their actions can have outsized consequences. In areas where they are just a tiny minority filled with vastly more characters, many of whom are much more powerful than them, their actions have much more minor consequences.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T12:11:30-07:00","id":1544742,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You're not listening to the points I made that the setting should in fact be impacted by the player's decisions and actions. I said that in both of my previous posts. So we're in agreement. </div> </div> </div>I'm not saying we're in total disagreement here, but the impression I got was that you said a story shouldn't have many changes to the setting, like having a clan &quot;exterminated&quot; (it's never really completed exterminated, but you get what I mean). So you gave me the impression that somethings should never change in a story in your point of view and with that I disagree.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is a huge difference between saying the PCs cannot make important decisions that affect the setting and that ONLY the PCs decisions are important because they can somehow cancel out the actions of all the other NPCs in the setting. In local and small scale areas where the PCs make up a significant faction of characters, their actions can have outsized consequences. In areas where they are just a tiny minority filled with vastly more characters, many of whom are much more powerful than them, their actions have much more minor consequences. </div> </div> </div>I'm not in any way saying that their actions are more important than that of the SPCs:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> and that of the SPCs they support </div> </div> </div>that's an important part I'm not sure you got what I really meant, perhaps I didn't express myself well (as I'm almost always in a hurry while writing here). What I mean is that the group of SPCs or NPCs they support can along with them bring major changes to the setting. If we take for instance a situation where a group support a powerful Methusaleh, it can bring major changes to the setting. If you have someone like Goratrix/Ur-Shulgi/Baba Yaga/Lazarus &quot;getting something they want&quot;, like for instance Lazarus wanted the Codex of Sargon to become godlike and found his own clan, just to use an example to make things clearer for us, then he's going to be able to bring major changes to the setting, he could even decide to end Augustus carreer (lol), to cite a perhaps &quot;silly example&quot;. I'm not with a lot of time to use perhaps more &quot;mundane&quot; examples, but I hope you get what I mean. It's not only the players, but their actions in support with that of a group of SPCs that can bring major changes to the setting and that's something quite interesting to play with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T12:15:51-07:00","id":1544744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This more scripted character design seems to be common if the Player is an ST. This gets more common if most if not all of your group are STs, since their minds are essentially trained to think in story arcs more than the experimental &quot;lets see what this does&quot; style of character design. </div> </div> </div>I agree with that, although I've always been a ST (if I've been a player 6 times over the last 20+ years that's a lot) and I love to play with changes, exactly because I don't like when I know exactly where we're headed in a story. I prefer to have a very reactive game where the choices of the players, naturally alongside that of a SPC or more often a group of them, can bring major changes to the setting when possible. Of course I know most ST can't really do it well, be it because they're not interested or not good at improvising such things and that's one of the reasons I started being a ST since I was a child.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> some Players want a Dark Souls experience where survival is the accomplishment, </div> </div> </div>As a Dark Souls lover myself, you can guess what kind of game my players usually have lol. But, I always like to say that it's possible to get what they want, but my games aren't &quot;easy&quot; at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T12:57:11-07:00","id":1544747,"title":"","content":"Another example, now that I found some time to write here is cited in Becket's diary, like Arthemis Orthia waking up and deciding to impose rules on the Camarilla as a whole. What I mean is that there are some characters that alone can change the whole setting and that, even more so with the help of a powerful group of players, can cause tremendous change to the setting.There are many such events and suggestions and possibilities in the writtings of the setting, the idea of the thread is to explore which changes have you had with your chronicles that span such a long time, but sometimes even a somwhat short chronicle can have something like that happening and it's interesting to see and talk about it, ofc I know most ST aren't really used to implement them (or even able to properly do so), but the discussion is potentially very interesting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T20:40:17-07:00","id":1544770,"title":"","content":"<br /> That's what I like about the Medieval-ages; I have 500-years to play around with; that's the life-span of an elder-vampire. Kings come and go; towns &amp; villages come and go, and yes, even vampires come and go. In a Dark Ages game, I started prior to the Norman-invasion, time when on, but, things stayed the same, for the most part.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-25T22:34:22-07:00","id":1544776,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544770#post1544770\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's what I like about the Medieval-ages; I have 500-years to play around with; that's the life-span of an elder-vampire. Kings come and go; towns &amp; villages come and go, and yes, even vampires come and go. In a Dark Ages game, I started prior to the Norman-invasion, time when on, but, things stayed the same, for the most part.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's also one of the main reasons I love to ST Dark Ages games. Also because in my opinion vampires lose a lot of its charms in the end of the 20th century and beyond, just to think of vampires with smartphones makes me sick lol","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-26T03:03:45-07:00","id":1544785,"title":"","content":"Perhaps it is just the people I have played with, but VtM set in the dark ages or earlier always seems to devolve into an Exalted Abyssal type of game. Oddly enough, the same thing happens if you set VtM in the post apocalypse/gehenna world.<br /> <br /> Personally, I had a lot of fun running pre-history games with VtM. The lower generations and added bonus discipline dots really lets you try out more stuff, while at the same time the lack of technology changes the combat meta to a significant degree. Running around &quot;<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6bekli_Tepe\" target=\"_blank\">Gobekli Tepe</a>&quot; during its active period between 9500 BCE and 8000 BCE means that shifters and mages are a lot scarier, but at the same time how the clans operate are also quite different, not to mention some clans or bloodlines don't exist yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-26T08:02:13-07:00","id":1544795,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Perhaps it is just the people I have played with, but VtM set in the dark ages or earlier always seems to devolve into an Exalted Abyssal type of game. Oddly enough, the same thing happens if you set VtM in the post apocalypse/gehenna world. </div> </div> </div>Well, sometimes, at least nowadays my games are mostly politics and social with the ocasional &quot;exalted moments&quot; during some fights, some of my more combat oriented players often &quot;complain&quot; that we go 3-4 game sessions without a single combat. They don't really &quot;complain&quot; per se, but it's easy to notice some comments that make me realize that perhaps it's time for another combat. One thing in particular though is that I hate &quot;random combat&quot;, if there are combats in my game, they're most of the time to conclude some dispute of two opposing sides in a given conflict, but I'm really not fond of that idea of &quot;you go into the forest...then you're surrounded by (faceless) werewolves&quot;. As I sometimes openly tell my players, I almost never let a PC die at the hands of a &quot;faceless NPC&quot;, if it happens (as it naturally does sooner or later in such long chronicles), it's goig to happen in a conflict with one of the important characters in the setting, be it to resolve some conflict or because sometimes PCs act in a not so clever way and provoke the ire of a powerful and known SPC...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-26T18:28:57-07:00","id":1544828,"title":"","content":"Well, like I briefly mentioned, I played a game upon which we were all 4th gen at the second city.<br /> <br /> My very own character was a former exiled prince, ambitious and merciless, you know, the sort of narcissist prick that believes the world is his birth right. He was to be the rightful heir of the throne, but as a tale old as time, got replaced by his sister nonetheless. All because the other members of the family thought that my nice little prince would become a tyrant or something (and he absolutely would).<br /> <br /> Well, he then had to flee for his life. With nothing else going for him, with no allies, no safe heavens, exiled from his own land and a stranger without connections on foreign lands, he decided to try his luck out based on some crazy rumours he heard about the &quot;city of dark magic&quot;, and he decided that he would pledge his entire kingdom to any dark wizard that could help him enact revenge on his usurper sister and traitorous family and servants.<br /> <br /> And so he went, until he finally came at the gates of Enoch, basically as a homeless person.<br /> <br /> Despite his pitiful state however, he was still a prince, and determined to get what he came for, he started to work towards getting to know such strange and imposing kingdom.<br /> <br /> The fact that he had a massive silver tongue didn't hurt either.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, we all began playing as mortals. And we actually played quite a handful of sessions as mortals, each one of us, and neither one of us really decided which clan we would be, that was decided upon us by the masters of the city.<br /> <br /> So, I managed to get a job with a merchant, and my skills amazed my employer, so I was able to accommodate myself fairly easy. And at all of my empty time I would try to find out any information that I could about the &quot;Dark Magicians&quot; that ruled the city.<br /> <br /> Eventually, I learned out about the clan Lasombra, and thus I decided that they would be perfect for me to pledge my allegiance (no, my character didn't know what a &quot;clan&quot; was, nor what the &quot;Lasombra&quot; really were; for him, they could just as well be some sort of &quot;guild&quot; of these magicians or something).<br /> <br /> Therefore, he tried to make his move, to directly approach these &quot;nobles&quot; and make a case for them to accept him as a member or at least a vassal.<br /> <br /> That... Didn't work quite like I expected. Little did I know that my former home was known by the &quot;dark wizards&quot; of Enoch, and they were not on good stands; there were many things running on the background by the part of the ST that I didn't know about.<br /> <br /> Therefore, someone from my kingdom approaching the Lasombra... Settled some fetters. And a few of the Antidiluvians came to &quot;extract&quot; information from me.<br /> <br /> To be very specific, SET FUCKING HIMSELF came to torture the truth out of me. And as a mere mortal being tortured by freaking Set, I quickly spilled my beans, I was trying to deceive them in order to help me get my revenge.<br /> <br /> Upon hearing this, Set immediately set me free and got absolutely delighted. &quot;REVENGE!&quot; He claimed with a smile upon his face. &quot;Why didn't you just say so? Hahaha&quot; he laughed in a diabolic tone.<br /> <br /> Then, something I was not expecting happened. You see, upon searching about the &quot;dark wizards&quot; houses, I thought the Lasombra would be perfect for my purposes.<br /> <br /> However, it was Set that... Set his eyes on me. And I became his property.<br /> <br /> He turned me into his ghoul (yep, I was still not a vampire, and that's several sessions of game play already), and introduced me into the clan.<br /> <br /> At first I was... Shocked. That was... Not what I had signed for.<br /> <br /> You see, I was a wannabe tyrant, but that's because my character was a narcissist that believe he knew better than anyone else.<br /> <br /> But his purpose with his relentless tyranny was to make his kingdom greater.<br /> <br /> What Set offered thou, was just decadence.<br /> <br /> But then Set said &quot;this is the natural state of things. In due time, everybody gets corrupted. Give it a try, even the noblest soul will fall under the right temptations. Your pathetic attempts to improve society is just an escape from the truth. All kingdoms become decadent and crumble in the end&quot;.<br /> <br /> Taking these words to mind, I decided to put it to a test. Despite getting into the city as a homeless, I was a skilled and merciless merchant who weren't shy to use some cutthroat methods when necessary to get things my way, therefore at that point I had already amassed a considerable amount of wealth.<br /> <br /> Thus, I set a plan to test Set's truths; I approached a poor peasant with a beautiful daughter, I obviously used my resources to look for someone with undoubted honor and honesty, who were considered as a pillar of the community and admired by his peers, and with a daughter that was deemed by all as a model of purity and chastity.<br /> <br /> Thus, I approached this man, posing as and honourable rich merchant (and the rich part was true), to propose to marry his daughter. It took some convincing, but after showing beyond any doubt that I was an honourable and honest man, he finally accepted the marriage proposal.<br /> <br /> Thus, I took my new beloved wife to my estate and... Began her &quot;processing&quot;.<br /> <br /> I forced her to lay down with other men. With slaves. With women. Even with animals.<br /> <br /> I was expecting her to break at any point and finally succumb to total debauchery. But. That. Never. Happened.<br /> <br /> Not just that, despite hating all the things I did to her, she still never moved against me.<br /> <br /> And... That changed me. No. Set was wrong.<br /> <br /> Well, meanwhile, my whole love story was not the only thing happening on the city. First of all, Cain - yes, the very first generation indeed. We actually met him - finally vanished for good.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, I had also met my other table friends. They were all vampires by now, I was the only ghoul left. Asdif the wise (Capadocian), Panthaleon the warrior (Gangrel) and Nonain (the only female player). Actually, Nonain was NOT a vampire yet, she was still fully mortal, being courted by the clan of Roses, but she at all the time refused to accept the dark gift, and rumours started to spread that Absimilliard was starting to take an interest on her.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I became good friends with these, and managed to make myself a sort of permanent resident of their house.<br /> <br /> At some point, they were out the house, and I got visited by a certain Nergal and a certain Shaitan... Yeah, that's right. They wanted me to... Deliver a certain message to Asdif and Panthaleon. They said they were planning something big, and wanted their help.<br /> <br /> Eventually, talking with this guys, I ended up talking too much and spilling the fact that I believed that Set was wrong... Which they delighted to hear (now, of course I didn't willingly confess that to them, but a recently formed ghoul have little chance to resist 2 4th gens, so they simply forced it out of me). They then fully supported me, and claimed that the Antidiluvians were oppressors that should be deposed, and that was their purpose, and that if I managed to do so I would be doing the right thing and whatnot, and that's why as a friend of Asdif and Panthaleon I could help to bring them to the cause and whatnot.<br /> <br /> Thus, I went with my wife out of the city, in search of these two.<br /> <br /> Set had given me a barrel with his vitae thou... But I never ceased learning.<br /> <br /> Believing the time to be of essence, I told my wife to drop the vitae on my mouth once my heart stopped, and then stabbed myself in the heart.<br /> <br /> And my beloved and obedient wife did as I ask, despite not knowing what was going on.<br /> <br /> And that's the story of how my character became a vampire under the city of Enoch<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-27T11:10:50-07:00","id":1544857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544776#post1544776\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's also one of the main reasons I love to ST Dark Ages games. Also because in my opinion vampires lose a lot of its charms in the end of the 20th century and beyond, just to think of vampires with smartphones makes me sick lol</div> </div> </div> </div>Cyberpunk's 2020: <i>Night's Edge</i> had specific-rules for vampires and cyberware; and only vampires of a certain AL(Age-Level(Young)), could have cyberware, but, if you had a cyberarm, you wouldn't grow back an arm every night, you'd have a permanent wound, I think. Anyways, in that game, the older you are, the more powerful you are. To change it up, I ran my fellow V;tM PCs in a CP:2020: NE game, because it was different as set in the past. We were teenagers when I ran it and I had only limited historical-knowledge (from a book I read at the local uni-library about Russia) so I kinda didn't enforce the anachronisms with a vampire using a cellphone like a should of ('80's version, they didn't have smart-phones in CP: 2020). The PCs' mortal years were spent fearing the wrath of Ivan the Terrible; they spend their neonate-years with pro-Tsar Russian vampires in Moscow, where they only embrace when a new Tsar is in power, hence one of the player's name was Lance of Ivan the Terrible. Back then, there was no Camarilla, just a bunch of vampires, with a vampire-prince, in a city and the wilderness beyond. The main PC didn't like the game at first because he said his Gangrel was Romanian. I said, yeah, your mother was Romanian and forced to married a boyar. <br /> <br /> While running the game, I had to be careful because the game-system was for more lethal than V:tM, but, PC's had true immortality. If they are trapped in a burning building, get burned alive and their ashes aren't disturbed, they'll come back, when they heal enough points to get there; as I remember, the game had an advanced than a mortal, but slower than V:tM's healing with blood-points and it got faster with age and the vampires skin would turn to armor.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-28T17:17:50-07:00","id":1544944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544828#post1544828\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, like I briefly mentioned, I played a game upon which we were all 4th gen at the second city.<br /> <br /> My very own character was a former exiled prince, ambitious and merciless, you know, the sort of narcissist prick that believes the world is his birth right. He was to be the rightful heir of the throne, but as a tale old as time, got replaced by his sister nonetheless. All because the other members of the family thought that my nice little prince would become a tyrant or something (and he absolutely would).<br /> <br /> Well, he then had to flee for his life. With nothing else going for him, with no allies, no safe heavens, exiled from his own land and a stranger without connections on foreign lands, he decided to try his luck out based on some crazy rumours he heard about the &quot;city of dark magic&quot;, and he decided that he would pledge his entire kingdom to any dark wizard that could help him enact revenge on his usurper sister and traitorous family and servants.<br /> <br /> And so he went, until he finally came at the gates of Enoch, basically as a homeless person.<br /> <br /> Despite his pitiful state however, he was still a prince, and determined to get what he came for, he started to work towards getting to know such strange and imposing kingdom.<br /> <br /> The fact that he had a massive silver tongue didn't hurt either.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, we all began playing as mortals. And we actually played quite a handful of sessions as mortals, each one of us, and neither one of us really decided which clan we would be, that was decided upon us by the masters of the city.<br /> <br /> So, I managed to get a job with a merchant, and my skills amazed my employer, so I was able to accommodate myself fairly easy. And at all of my empty time I would try to find out any information that I could about the &quot;Dark Magicians&quot; that ruled the city.<br /> <br /> Eventually, I learned out about the clan Lasombra, and thus I decided that they would be perfect for me to pledge my allegiance (no, my character didn't know what a &quot;clan&quot; was, nor what the &quot;Lasombra&quot; really were; for him, they could just as well be some sort of &quot;guild&quot; of these magicians or something).<br /> <br /> Therefore, he tried to make his move, to directly approach these &quot;nobles&quot; and make a case for them to accept him as a member or at least a vassal.<br /> <br /> That... Didn't work quite like I expected. Little did I know that my former home was known by the &quot;dark wizards&quot; of Enoch, and they were not on good stands; there were many things running on the background by the part of the ST that I didn't know about.<br /> <br /> Therefore, someone from my kingdom approaching the Lasombra... Settled some fetters. And a few of the Antidiluvians came to &quot;extract&quot; information from me.<br /> <br /> To be very specific, SET FUCKING HIMSELF came to torture the truth out of me. And as a mere mortal being tortured by freaking Set, I quickly spilled my beans, I was trying to deceive them in order to help me get my revenge.<br /> <br /> Upon hearing this, Set immediately set me free and got absolutely delighted. &quot;REVENGE!&quot; He claimed with a smile upon his face. &quot;Why didn't you just say so? Hahaha&quot; he laughed in a diabolic tone.<br /> <br /> Then, something I was not expecting happened. You see, upon searching about the &quot;dark wizards&quot; houses, I thought the Lasombra would be perfect for my purposes.<br /> <br /> However, it was Set that... Set his eyes on me. And I became his property.<br /> <br /> He turned me into his ghoul (yep, I was still not a vampire, and that's several sessions of game play already), and introduced me into the clan.<br /> <br /> At first I was... Shocked. That was... Not what I had signed for.<br /> <br /> You see, I was a wannabe tyrant, but that's because my character was a narcissist that believe he knew better than anyone else.<br /> <br /> But his purpose with his relentless tyranny was to make his kingdom greater.<br /> <br /> What Set offered thou, was just decadence.<br /> <br /> But then Set said &quot;this is the natural state of things. In due time, everybody gets corrupted. Give it a try, even the noblest soul will fall under the right temptations. Your pathetic attempts to improve society is just an escape from the truth. All kingdoms become decadent and crumble in the end&quot;.<br /> <br /> Taking these words to mind, I decided to put it to a test. Despite getting into the city as a homeless, I was a skilled and merciless merchant who weren't shy to use some cutthroat methods when necessary to get things my way, therefore at that point I had already amassed a considerable amount of wealth.<br /> <br /> Thus, I set a plan to test Set's truths; I approached a poor peasant with a beautiful daughter, I obviously used my resources to look for someone with undoubted honor and honesty, who were considered as a pillar of the community and admired by his peers, and with a daughter that was deemed by all as a model of purity and chastity.<br /> <br /> Thus, I approached this man, posing as and honourable rich merchant (and the rich part was true), to propose to marry his daughter. It took some convincing, but after showing beyond any doubt that I was an honourable and honest man, he finally accepted the marriage proposal.<br /> <br /> Thus, I took my new beloved wife to my estate and... Began her &quot;processing&quot;.<br /> <br /> I forced her to lay down with other men. With slaves. With women. Even with animals.<br /> <br /> I was expecting her to break at any point and finally succumb to total debauchery. But. That. Never. Happened.<br /> <br /> Not just that, despite hating all the things I did to her, she still never moved against me.<br /> <br /> And... That changed me. No. Set was wrong.<br /> <br /> Well, meanwhile, my whole love story was not the only thing happening on the city. First of all, Cain - yes, the very first generation indeed. We actually met him - finally vanished for good.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, I had also met my other table friends. They were all vampires by now, I was the only ghoul left. Asdif the wise (Capadocian), Panthaleon the warrior (Gangrel) and Nonain (the only female player). Actually, Nonain was NOT a vampire yet, she was still fully mortal, being courted by the clan of Roses, but she at all the time refused to accept the dark gift, and rumours started to spread that Absimilliard was starting to take an interest on her.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I became good friends with these, and managed to make myself a sort of permanent resident of their house.<br /> <br /> At some point, they were out the house, and I got visited by a certain Nergal and a certain Shaitan... Yeah, that's right. They wanted me to... Deliver a certain message to Asdif and Panthaleon. They said they were planning something big, and wanted their help.<br /> <br /> Eventually, talking with this guys, I ended up talking too much and spilling the fact that I believed that Set was wrong... Which they delighted to hear (now, of course I didn't willingly confess that to them, but a recently formed ghoul have little chance to resist 2 4th gens, so they simply forced it out of me). They then fully supported me, and claimed that the Antidiluvians were oppressors that should be deposed, and that was their purpose, and that if I managed to do so I would be doing the right thing and whatnot, and that's why as a friend of Asdif and Panthaleon I could help to bring them to the cause and whatnot.<br /> <br /> Thus, I went with my wife out of the city, in search of these two.<br /> <br /> Set had given me a barrel with his vitae thou... But I never ceased learning.<br /> <br /> Believing the time to be of essence, I told my wife to drop the vitae on my mouth once my heart stopped, and then stabbed myself in the heart.<br /> <br /> And my beloved and obedient wife did as I ask, despite not knowing what was going on.<br /> <br /> And that's the story of how my character became a vampire under the city of Enoch<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b<br /> <br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an interesting tale. In fact, I've never dared storytelling a story in Enoch itself, the closer any of my characters have been is to the Necropolis of the Tal'Mahe'Ra in the Shadowlands (not quite the same as going there while it was thriving with unlife). I've always felt Enoch and Antediluvians should remain &quot;mysteryous&quot; and distant from the players. What I did once, a long time ago was storytelling a story in ancient greece and then Constantinople later in the same story, the characters ended in Constantinople around 1000 AD, because they died there and I felt I had enough of the story. From then on I've only focused in middle ages, I feel dark ages is a more interesting setting overall, but that is due to personal taste the same that makes me (most of the time at least) end the story before the 20th century or make something so bizarre as to make technology not advance much. I think I have some Tradition Mage from Sorcerer's Crusade bias hahaha...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-28T17:19:11-07:00","id":1544945,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Iguazu</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544857#post1544857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Cyberpunk's 2020: <i>Night's Edge</i> had specific-rules for vampires and cyberware; and only vampires of a certain AL(Age-Level(Young)), could have cyberware, but, if you had a cyberarm, you wouldn't grow back an arm every night, you'd have a permanent wound, I think. Anyways, in that game, the older you are, the more powerful you are. To change it up, I ran my fellow V;tM PCs in a CP:2020: NE game, because it was different as set in the past. We were teenagers when I ran it and I had only limited historical-knowledge (from a book I read at the local uni-library about Russia) so I kinda didn't enforce the anachronisms with a vampire using a cellphone like a should of ('80's version, they didn't have smart-phones in CP: 2020). The PCs' mortal years were spent fearing the wrath of Ivan the Terrible; they spend their neonate-years with pro-Tsar Russian vampires in Moscow, where they only embrace when a new Tsar is in power, hence one of the player's name was Lance of Ivan the Terrible. Back then, there was no Camarilla, just a bunch of vampires, with a vampire-prince, in a city and the wilderness beyond. The main PC didn't like the game at first because he said his Gangrel was Romanian. I said, yeah, your mother was Romanian and forced to married a boyar.<br /> <br /> While running the game, I had to be careful because the game-system was for more lethal than V:tM, but, PC's had true immortality. If they are trapped in a burning building, get burned alive and their ashes aren't disturbed, they'll come back, when they heal enough points to get there; as I remember, the game had an advanced than a mortal, but slower than V:tM's healing with blood-points and it got faster with age and the vampires skin would turn to armor.</div> </div> </div> </div>Cyberpunk I feel is a totally different beast. In the sense that I don't have a particular problem with this kind of thing. I just think that vampire themes in general don't work well (for me) with &quot;too much technology&quot;. It's personal taste.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-29T13:39:01-07:00","id":1545005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544945#post1544945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Cyberpunk I feel is a totally different beast. In the sense that I don't have a particular problem with this kind of thing. I just think that vampire themes in general don't work well (for me) with &quot;too much technology&quot;. It's personal taste.</div> </div> </div> </div>How do you feel about Vampires being the preservers of technology? Such as in Vampire Hunter D, Trinity Blood, or the Castlevania series?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-29T20:18:02-07:00","id":1545032,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544944#post1544944\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's an interesting tale. In fact, I've never dared storytelling a story in Enoch itself, the closer any of my characters have been is to the Necropolis of the Tal'Mahe'Ra in the Shadowlands (not quite the same as going there while it was thriving with unlife). I've always felt Enoch and Antediluvians should remain &quot;mysteryous&quot; and distant from the players. What I did once, a long time ago was storytelling a story in ancient greece and then Constantinople later in the same story, the characters ended in Constantinople around 1000 AD, because they died there and I felt I had enough of the story. From then on I've only focused in middle ages, I feel dark ages is a more interesting setting overall, but that is due to personal taste the same that makes me (most of the time at least) end the story before the 20th century or make something so bizarre as to make technology not advance much. I think I have some Tradition Mage from Sorcerer's Crusade bias hahaha...</div> </div> </div> </div>That was just the beginning of my story. I ended up &quot;breaking up&quot; with daddy Set, took tutelage under Saulot, joined the Children of Osiris under the wing of Horus himself, but eventually left them too, considering the Children hell bent on vengeance, and went towards my path to Golconda. I embraced my wife and the son we had, and I made my mortal descendants mix with the descendants of the other player Panthaleon and I kept tabs of them throughout the ages as a sort of pastime, then I ended up killing my wife to free her from Set - he captured her to try to force me to bow. But, with huge pain in my heart, I killed her with a kiss (which ended with the snake tongue piercing her skull), so I set both her and myself free from Set, after which I had no more reason to exist, and Set let me go.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, I also kind of invented both the Jyhad and the Inconnu... Well, remember all the prophecies of the book of Nod? Well... That was... Kinda of my creation... You know, in our game, those things weren't really real... At least, not until I made them real.<br /> <br /> Because, the prophecies of the book of Nod (which I created) would become self fulfilling prophecies... Which was my intent all along.<br /> <br /> And why? Well, if I tell the neonates that their elders are up to get them, then surely these neonates will try to act against their elders... But the elders, knowing that the neonates want to act against them, will surely act against the neonates.<br /> <br /> That's how I basically created the Jyhad on my table. By creating a fabricated lie, but a lie that becomes true due to its vicious cycle.<br /> <br /> And why did I do that?<br /> <br /> Because I wanted to remove the elders from the game. I wanted to create the Inconnu. Which I did. And I wanted the Inconnu so I could manipulate the elders.<br /> <br /> Yeah, that was a crazy table, so memorable. We also got into Greece, Rome, Carthage and many many eras around.<br /> <br /> The Antidiluvians we just met briefly, but we did interact quite a bit with many of the famous 4th and 5th gens of note. I was particularly found of Nosferati and became good friends with Baba Yaga... Yeah, long story.<br /> <br /> And, in all of the many years we played this, I never got into combat, and the only other person I ever killed was my wife.<br /> <br /> I was a master manipulator at that game","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-29T20:42:40-07:00","id":1545035,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544945#post1544945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Cyberpunk I feel is a totally different beast. In the sense that I don't have a particular problem with this kind of thing. I just think that vampire themes in general don't work well (for me) with &quot;too much technology&quot;. It's personal taste.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find Space Opera Vampire to be particularly fun, even more so than Cyberpunk. It's entirely different from the &quot;typical&quot; games around the subject, verging on the comic aspect rather than &quot;uh, depression and horror all the time&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-29T21:00:10-07:00","id":1545037,"title":"","content":"Just to finish my tale about the second city game I played, I can count on my fingers the times I used any Discipline at all. And I never engaged in combat. It was &quot;talking simulator&quot; as D&amp;D detractors would claim lol<br /> <br /> And the game went over the span of several millennia. Yeah, I had all my social and mental attributes at 9 (my intelligence and manipulation were 10, since my ST allowed me to buy epic attributes merit), several disciplines at 9 or 10 - I reached Golconda. At lvl 10 I had Presence, Obfuscation, Auspex and Thaumaturgy (Blood Sorcery). Yes, I was a master thaumaturge. We were getting close to the medieval times, and I had already talked about it with the ST, I wanted to know if Saulot would ask me to help the rising Tremere, and he said he would - remember, Saulot was once my mentor, and I had a veneration towards him as a father figure. I was eager to teach those fledgling upstarts &quot;Tremere&quot; bloodline a few tricks regarding Blood Magic, and in my mind I had no doubts that I would be the one to &quot;leak&quot; to them the information about Saulot's resting place, but unfortunately the table dispersed when we were close to getting there.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, I also tried to advice the Troileans (the &quot;Brujah&quot;, but the guys my age refused to call the usurpers that way) not to antagonize Rome, but they didn't listen. I was their advisor and I was also the mentor of Catherina the Wise (she stole my Surname, &quot;the Wise&quot; was how I was called), but even with her voice echoing mine it was in vain. So I helped her escape the inevitable destruction of Carthage and she became my foster child - the other guys they... They had killed Troille. Way before the fall of Carthage. So, she was an orphan.<br /> <br /> Yep. The players at that table killed a third generation. That's how powerful we were. Granted, Troile was the same age as myself. I didn't participate thou, because I was sort of the Buddha of the Vampires, so I just watched.<br /> <br /> Edit: about Thaumaturgy, I was sooooo much better at it than Tremere ever would be. Only Baba Yaga surpassed me. And she was my friend. That's how ridiculously powerful that table was. And yet, it was NEVER about the power. Like I said, all I ever did was... Talk. Lol.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28035,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","user":"Magus Maximus","date":". 10-30-2024, 12:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-31T08:11:39-07:00","id":1545093,"title":"","content":"This is so gonzo that I can't help but feel that you're trolling, as I don't think any half serious attempt at storytelling would have a story so absurd and ridiculous as this one or allow a player to have level 10 in a discipline.<br /> In any case, just to enlight you, according to the setting, Tremere Thaumaturgy is on another level compared to Baba Yaga. She has &quot;just&quot; level 9 if you ever cared to see her character's sheet. Not that any of this would have any relevance in a story so absurd as the one you shared here. You're clearly trolling.<br /> But congratulations, you made me regret starting this thread lol...<br /> <br /> In any case, as you're clearly not only trolling, but also kind of spamming. I wonder why no moderator came here, but went to an interesting discussion (alabei it did have quite some cursing eexchanges) in the thread &quot;Do you like Paradox&quot; on the Mage's subforum...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 10-31-2024, 11:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-31T08:13:25-07:00","id":1545094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545005#post1545005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> How do you feel about Vampires being the preservers of technology? Such as in Vampire Hunter D, Trinity Blood, or the Castlevania series?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I feel like it's a totally different concept. At least in Castlevania, I don't know much about the others, but technology in Castlevania seems very different from &quot;having smartphones&quot;. So then, especially in that universe, I don't have any problem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-01T03:04:01-07:00","id":1545127,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545094#post1545094\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, I feel like it's a totally different concept. At least in Castlevania, I don't know much about the others, but technology in Castlevania seems very different from &quot;having smartphones&quot;. So then, especially in that universe, I don't have any problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough. In both Trinity Blood and Vampire Hunter D the world was post apocalypse.<br /> <br /> In Vampire Hunter D the world functioned like a cross between the old west and medieval Europe with peasants working farms and keeping a rifle nearby to deal with predators, but the horses are cyborgs. The vampires function as the nobility but they tend to maintain technology from before the new dark age (5k to 10k years ago) like having personal space ships and high tech castles.<br /> <br /> Trinity Blood on the other hand had vampires show up out of nowhere and take over about half the planet, with the other half being controlled by the church. Such a calamity caused the mortal populace to backslide when it came to technology with only the highest levels of the church being high technology, but the vampires maintain nanite level technology in their society.<br /> <br /> In a couple of post Gehenna games I STed in the distant past, I ran with the idea of the PCs being from our time and trying to carve out a niche for themselves in a post Gehenna world. Interestingly enough the Players usually ended up creating some form of city state and used their own knowledge to create a vampiric social class that were lore keepers and scientists trying to preserve knowledge and regain some of what was lost.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-01T06:53:43-07:00","id":1545131,"title":"","content":"One thing that I would like to see is a &quot;Vampire Post-Apocalyptic World&quot;. As in, something like the Sabbath &quot;wins&quot; and then &quot;unleashes&quot; itself upon the world, causing society to collapse. Just changing Zombies for Vampires. So, you'd have vampires sleeping at caves, human survivors trying to &quot;clear&quot; these caves during the day and everything in between","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-01T13:02:19-07:00","id":1545147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545131#post1545131\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing that I would like to see is a &quot;Vampire Post-Apocalyptic World&quot;. As in, something like the Sabbath &quot;wins&quot; and then &quot;unleashes&quot; itself upon the world, causing society to collapse. Just changing Zombies for Vampires. So, you'd have vampires sleeping at caves, human survivors trying to &quot;clear&quot; these caves during the day and everything in between</div> </div> </div> </div>One Gehenna game I ran had the Giovanni pull off their plan to bring down the barrier between the mortal realm and the shadowlands. But there was a hitch, rather than merge the two realms, it just made the relatively fresh dead rise as zombies all across the world. I ran the zombies like the World War Z types which moved relatively quickly and thus the world collapsed. But here is the twist, the zombies don't react to vampires unless attacked. So now the vampire community is stuck in an interesting position where they have to gather up and protect the surviving humans just so they have a food supply in these final nights..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-01T20:25:50-07:00","id":1545189,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544945#post1544945\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Cyberpunk I feel is a totally different beast. In the sense that I don't have a particular problem with this kind of thing. I just think that vampire themes in general don't work well (for me) with &quot;too much technology&quot;. It's personal taste.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am in agreement; and what's worse, is trying to adapt the Cyberpunk rule-system to the past; the Interlock system is awesome, but it doesn't have that old tyme feel that Vampire: the Masquerade has. <br /> <br /> It's was difficult to run a game in 1500's Russia with a Cyberpunk: 2020 sheet, so, I redesigned it for the past. Still, I lament.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;I have all the power in the world to give you, and all you have to do is beg&quot; -Nix<br /> <br /> &quot;There's thing about being a vampire, Frank; it takes a long fucking time to learn how do all the cool shit&quot;. -Abigail","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2521-iguazu","name":"Iguazu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2521_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"738"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-06T03:45:41-08:00","id":1545454,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1544584#post1544584\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Breaking or changing the setting happens almost automatically if you are not artificially enforcing the outcome.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is a very important point to remember, one of the main reasons VtM in particular and WoD in general are so popular is because they 'enforce' the various <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/TabletopGame/VampireTheMasquerade\" target=\"_blank\">tropes </a>the game is themed around.<br /> <br /> Thus, when you accidentally or deliberately move away from those tropes things suddenly stop making sense because the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suspension_of_disbelief\" target=\"_blank\">suspension of disbelief</a> is broken.<br /> <br /> VtM only works as a setting when the world revolves around the vampires in a certain way, so, when building your own canon, you need to be mindful of the associated tropes and make sure not to break them.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-06T06:30:37-08:00","id":1545462,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545454#post1545454\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">VtM only works as a setting when the world revolves around the vampires in a certain way, so, when building your own canon, you need to be mindful of the associated tropes and make sure not to break them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes and no. You can maintain the feel, the genre, and the tropes all while changing the numbers around. For example,<br /> <br /> City A: Fluff typical vampire city with many if not most clans and bloodlines represented, all struggling against each other to maintain or gain power while surviving the next thing that goes wrong.<br /> <br /> City B: Mono-Clan city where only one clan/bloodline resides there permanently. While there may be the occasional power struggle between clan mates, the true danger comes from outside the city from the other clans, other city states, or other supernatural's.<br /> <br /> The first city is what you see in almost every VtM book in an attempt to front load as many plot hooks and ideas as possible to get new players creative juices flowing and make first time STs have more of a &quot;turn-key&quot; experience. The second city keeps all the threats the same, but changes things from &quot;what promotes story stuff&quot; to &quot;how would that work best while maintaining the vibe&quot;.<br /> <br /> It is an example of how you can modulate the power of characters without making changes to the actual mechanics balance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-07T09:53:56-08:00","id":1545525,"title":"","content":"I've been narrating Transylvania Chronicles (1198) and Giovanni Chronicles (1444) in alternate with the same players and different PCs. We are currently in 1600, after I made a homebrew Promisse of 1528 chronicle. In the way to the 2nd book of GC (and then we will go to the 3rd book of TC). With the Transylvania group we also played Under the Black Cross, and had interludes involved with the Dark Ages Novel Saga threads. <br /> <br /> I can say that before character are Elders I find very hard to truly have divergence from the canon. And the books don't help, they will spiel to hell and back how 'you can do whatyou want' but don't give not a single tip or help as how to it. They are structured as rigidly and narrowly as they come. And many 'choices' or 'crossroads' are freaking irrelevant. Like do you want to destroy or save the Eldest Tzimisce? Who cares, the damn thing will live by its own machinations either way. <br /> <br /> But, when the PCs are Elders they should be able to plan-out and execute changes in their sects, their cities, and the metaplot. The damn system doesn't help that at all, neither does the chronicle books or the metaplot really (many finer but important things that we should know are not found anywhere). <br /> When the PCs are not Elders, they should take part in big changes and have agency in tilting the scales, or making some momentous decisions in the thick of it. Elders can plan and execute their changes, Ancilla and Neonates can do it in the moment. <br /> <br /> I find it important to each game table customize their own World of Darkness setting. Give it their own aesthetic, their own plot, their own cointinuity. Otherwise it can feel too tied-down by the real world (and feel just mundane and static instead of living and changing) or an ideal metaplot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Strange... When coincidence seems too convenient, I prefer to call it fate.</i><br /> <br /> <b><sup>-Blood Omen: Legacy of Kain</sup> </b>d=","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25534-banu_saulot","name":"Banu_Saulot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25534_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"335"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-11-07T12:00:06-08:00","id":1545529,"title":"","content":"Here's a couple of ideas I've been toying with for some time, but never managed to incorporate into a chronicle:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Folding the Anarchs into the Sabbat\u2014as in, the Anarchs are now the form assumed by the Sabbat in those cities where it has yet to establish itself as the dominant faction. Instead of Revised era's &quot;sect wars&quot;, every territorial concession the Camarilla makes to the Sabbat assumes the appearance of yet another Revolt: instead of one, two or three Anarch Revolts, there's a hundred small revolts taking place every time young &amp; discontent undead decide to oust the local Camarilla. I imagine Sabbat/Anarch control over cities lasts relatively briefly after each revolt, perhaps concurrently with periods of relative decadence and infrastructure collapse (if one wishes to keep the setting &quot;true to life&quot;, of course).</li> <li>Resurrecting the V1 oddity of princes not being an inherent element of the Camarilla. I've always liked the depiction of undead society in <i>Constantinople by Night</i>\u2014it's the only sourcebook that managed to convince me its characters, and the constellation of character relations laid out in the book is unique to that city, instead of being a local variant on an established template (another Ventrue prince, and a Toreador harpy\u2014hooray...). Maybe this is because the sorucebook was a <i>Dark Ages</i> product, but I see no reason why you couldn't do something similar in a modern setting.</li> <li>Folding the Giovanni into the Lasombra\u2014or, rather, making the Giovanni the descendants of those Lasombra who did not join the First Anarch Revolt. The Giovanni's habit of only embracing family members would've originally been a characteristic of the Lasombra clan as a whole, abandoned by those members of the clan who went Anarch; maybe make them a Camarilla clan while you're at it, too. Also, perhaps doing something similar with the Gangrel and the Tzismice could be fun.</li> <li>Moving the date of the establishment of the Camarilla to the 1730s, the time of the Austrian vampire hysteria that brought knowledge of vampires to Western Europe (the word &quot;vampire&quot; apparently entered English in 1732). Also, the early 18th century is dead in the middle of the Age of Enlightenment and just a few decades before the start of the Atlantic Revolutions; I suppose the First Anarch Revolt could be seen as a sort-of undead analogue to ideological &amp; political changes in mortal society.</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28177-gamiem","name":"gamiem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28177_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-01T20:51:37-08:00","id":1546527,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545454#post1545454\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think this is a very important point to remember, one of the main reasons VtM in particular and WoD in general are so popular is because they 'enforce' the various <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/TabletopGame/VampireTheMasquerade\" target=\"_blank\">tropes </a>the game is themed around.<br /> <br /> Thus, when you accidentally or deliberately move away from those tropes things suddenly stop making sense because the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suspension_of_disbelief\" target=\"_blank\">suspension of disbelief</a> is broken.<br /> <br /> VtM only works as a setting when the world revolves around the vampires in a certain way, so, when building your own canon, you need to be mindful of the associated tropes and make sure not to break them.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I undrstand your point, but at the same time think it all depends (as most of the time) on the ST and the group of players, a good group and especially a good ST can make those changes and easily make it work. I don't see any problem in having the Giovanni totally obliteratted 5 sconds after they diablerize Capadocius, this wouldn't affect much the setting and in fact that's what they basically went for with V5, where it seems Lazarus just broke the clan, creating another (more interesting one, one of the few things V5 did right in this regard). Everything depends on how those things are handled. I made these things work for sereval years with different stories with different groups, in fact I have a group that I've been ST for the last 14 years and in some stories the Tzimisces were kind of destroyed (at least the Antideluvian and most important ones) and the story went on as normal. There are clans that affect the setting more than others. The stupid Second Inquisition thing V5 did with Vienna was likely the worst offender in this sense and still, it seems the setting kind of survived (even though not very well, I must add, but then it was more because the whole thing was rather ridiculous).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-04T20:53:57-08:00","id":1546623,"title":"","content":"I actually like the idea of the Kindred power structure was set up in Constinople by Night and we have that in our game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-05T08:05:48-08:00","id":1546640,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1546527#post1546527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I undrstand your point, but at the same time think it all depends (as most of the time) on the ST and the group of players, a good group and especially a good ST can make those changes and easily make it work. I don't see any problem in having the Giovanni totally obliteratted 5 sconds after they diablerize Capadocius, this wouldn't affect much the setting and in fact that's what they basically went for with V5, where it seems Lazarus just broke the clan, creating another (more interesting one, one of the few things V5 did right in this regard). Everything depends on how those things are handled. I made these things work for sereval years with different stories with different groups, in fact I have a group that I've been ST for the last 14 years and in some stories the Tzimisces were kind of destroyed (at least the Antideluvian and most important ones) and the story went on as normal. There are clans that affect the setting more than others. The stupid Second Inquisition thing V5 did with Vienna was likely the worst offender in this sense and still, it seems the setting kind of survived (even though not very well, I must add, but then it was more because the whole thing was rather ridiculous).</div> </div> </div> </div>That would still be within the confines of VtM. I think that what the OP to which you responded meant by was something like having the Inquisition be a creation of the Order of Reason and having vampires hiding from the Technocracy. Or having the Lupines fight the Technocracy, or having the Traditions having to fend off against the Sabbath or even the Camarilla. That's what I think it means to &quot;going off the script of the troupes&quot;.<br /> <br /> Or having cyberpunk vampires. That sort of thing would go so off the rails that you would have to basically reinvent the entire setting.<br /> <br /> On my &quot;Second City&quot; game for instance, the players at my table (all 4th gen with 1000+ years) actually killed Troile in a fist fight. Oh, and I became the mentor of Ecatherina the wise, we saw the Ventrue faking his own death, Set was permanently locked into the Abyss by the Lasombra, I accidentally started a war between the Gorgons (the sisters of Nosferati) and the Nosferatu and Baba Yaga against the Setites, and some more things (yes, it was totally accidental. I asked the 4th gen progeny of Absimilliard who was a player to shelter my progeny wife from Set, and the Setites stormed their keep in order to capture her). I also had plans to help out the Tremere, under the tutelage of my mentor Saulot, once the time came.<br /> <br /> Oh yes, I also basically invented Gehenna. Remember all that &quot;Book of Nod&quot; stuff? Well... As it turns out.. It was all part of a personal fabrication. I wanted to kinda of force elders to seek Golconda, thus I invented that story about &quot;the elders will rise and feed on the young!&quot;. The purpose was to get younger vampires to fear the elders, thus therefore making elders fear younglings, and thus having the elders segregated from vampiric society. And I created the Inconnu in earnest as a response - lived for centuries? Do you fear being eaten by the younglings? Join our club and fear not! We offer you salvation!<br /> <br /> Yes. A ton of problems in the Vampiric Society, and essentially the very existence of the Sabbath, all of that were due to a fabrication on my part, a master 4D chess game that I played. And I think that's very fitting for a Manipulation 10 Methuselah 4th gen with thousands of years. And after thousands of years, many vampires were still killing each other over it, but more importantly, many elders were seeking Golconda because of that &quot;noble lie&quot;.<br /> <br /> But the thing is, all of this changes were done within the context of VtM. There were no space wizards with light sabers disrupting the setting","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-05T08:09:54-08:00","id":1546641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>gamiem</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545529#post1545529\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's a couple of ideas I've been toying with for some time, but never managed to incorporate into a chronicle:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Folding the Anarchs into the Sabbat\u2014as in, the Anarchs are now the form assumed by the Sabbat in those cities where it has yet to establish itself as the dominant faction. Instead of Revised era's &quot;sect wars&quot;, every territorial concession the Camarilla makes to the Sabbat assumes the appearance of yet another Revolt: instead of one, two or three Anarch Revolts, there's a hundred small revolts taking place every time young &amp; discontent undead decide to oust the local Camarilla. I imagine Sabbat/Anarch control over cities lasts relatively briefly after each revolt, perhaps concurrently with periods of relative decadence and infrastructure collapse (if one wishes to keep the setting &quot;true to life&quot;, of course).</li> </ol></div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually a neat idea. I would place the &quot;Sabbath&quot; as a hidden extremist cult that constantly infiltrates these structures. Thus, you have revolts, some are organic, some others are instigated by these secret cultists, and after a successful revolt, the cult tries to recruit between the young and inexperienced anarchists. I would like to have such a setting","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T01:52:52-08:00","id":1546917,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1546527#post1546527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I undrstand your point, but at the same time think it all depends (as most of the time) on the ST and the group of players, a good group and especially a good ST can make those changes and easily make it work.<br /> I don't see any problem in...</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not quite sure you do understand completely - the lore changes you described, such as eleminating the Giovanni and Thoth's two cities don't appear to have any effect on the tropes themselves. Vampires still get destroyed by the sun, it's still a grimdark setting, angsty teen vamps are still dressing in trenchcoats and bemoaning their fate, etc.<br /> <br /> Magus Maximus' Second City game, as described, does appear to be one that breaks the tropes and stomps on the pieces though. Which, while I wouldn't be interested in that sort of a game, <i>doesn't make it any less valid</i>.<br /> <br /> As you point out, as long as the players and the ST are enjoying the story, and the trope breaking is part of it, then it works for that table.<br /> <br /> It's hard for most people to neatly delineate the lines between theme and setting. John wick, for example, is a quintessential WoD vampire story, except that there are no vampires in it, only criminals. Every element would fit so neatly into the VtM setting that it's astonishing. A similar issue applies to Furies, the French TV series that has a similar setting to John Wick with crime families running Paris. With a few tiny tweaks you could drop the whole thing into WoD. Both of these have themes that perfectly match VtM but settings that are a step away.<br /> <br /> I think I've started rambling, so I'll stop now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T19:29:49-08:00","id":1546947,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1546917#post1546917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Magus Maximus' Second City game, as described, does appear to be one that breaks the tropes and stomps on the pieces though. Which, while I wouldn't be interested in that sort of a game, <i>doesn't make it any less valid</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, we didn't play VtM at all, there was no princes and no Sabbath and no fearing our younglings because, honestly, f*ck them all, we were basically Gods in (in)human form, so it quickly turned into a game of high philosophy, and just simply about the tedium of spending millennia alive, and watching the world change.<br /> <br /> I've even kept a funny hobby, I &quot;collected&quot; my mortal descendants, I even mixed them (on purpose) with the mortal descendants of another player, and I kept tabs on all of them throughout the generations, just as a real hobby. There were really no power plays or power grabs, because all of those were so beneath us that simply didn't even make sense. It was a completely different game from angst toddler vamps vs cynical callous elders. Given how 5th gen for us were like toddlers, a city prince or the entire progeny were just mere insects. We would see the lesser younger vampires (as in, elders), as elders see the younglings and as those see the mortals.<br /> <br /> That's really a HUGE change, and some settings like &quot;vampire cyberpunk&quot; or better yet, &quot;space opera vampire&quot; would represent that kind of utter departure from such common trope","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T19:52:15-08:00","id":1546948,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545093#post1545093\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is so gonzo that I can't help but feel that you're trolling, as I don't think any half serious attempt at storytelling would have a story so absurd and ridiculous as this one or allow a player to have level 10 in a discipline.<br /> In any case, just to enlight you, according to the setting, Tremere Thaumaturgy is on another level compared to Baba Yaga. She has &quot;just&quot; level 9 if you ever cared to see her character's sheet. Not that any of this would have any relevance in a story so absurd as the one you shared here. You're clearly trolling.<br /> But congratulations, you made me regret starting this thread lol...<br /> <br /> In any case, as you're clearly not only trolling, but also kind of spamming. I wonder why no moderator came here, but went to an interesting discussion (alabei it did have quite some cursing eexchanges) in the thread &quot;Do you like Paradox&quot; on the Mage's subforum...</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm absolutely not trolling. We played that game for almost 4, on weekly appointments. And, there were several spaces of centuries between stories on occasions, upon which we would either sleep or just not do anything of significance. Hence why our character sheets were as good as if you had just dropped ink on them. And I was allowed to have Disciplines at lvl 10 because I actually reached Golconda. I was a disciple of Saulot after all, and I followed his footsteps. I had 2 attributes at lvl 10, Intelligence and Manipulation, with the rest of my Social and Mental attributes being at 9. For my 2 attributes at lvl 10, the ST allowed me to buy epic attribute for each.<br /> <br /> And no, I'm most definitely not trolling. We played the Second City, I personally met Cain (briefly) because it was a law in the city that every Cainite had to be introduced to him, but he just didn't care about anything at all, so I just said my name and clan and he waved for me to go, another player at the table actually insulted him, saying &quot;why do we still need to introduce ourselves to that farmer&quot;, not knowing he was the guy right next to him, he just asked his name and ordered him to go without any care at all (but both Ventrue and Saulot opened their eyes as if they were about to drop their skulls), then Cain left for good (he would always stay longs periods of time absent), then the city fell under Troile and Baali fights, and we saw Ventrue faking his death (which we never spoke about again), and then we kinda wandered the Earth for quite a long time, until eventually I decided to take root on Carthage AFTER my guys defeated Troile in combat - we were all roughly the same age as him after all, and we were 4 while he was only 1.<br /> <br /> All of these things happened. Then, I took the orphan lineage of the Troileans under my personal tutelage, considering them innocent from their progenitor's crimes, and I guided them up until they decided to ignore my advices and went to war with Rome.<br /> <br /> All of those things and many many more happened, we roleplayed all of that, our ST at the time did a fantastic job interpreting several of the Antidiluvians that we interacted with, including making them much more 3 dimensional than just simple alien mysteries, as well as several famous 4th gen from official fonts. Oh yes, and I became good buddies with Baba Yaga, whom I would always compliment on her incredible beauty.<br /> <br /> And I also wrote the Book of Nod as a sort of &quot;psyops&quot; to manipulate the entire Cainite society (that's the result of Intelligence 10 and Manipulation 10)<br /> <br /> I don't know why would you think this is trolling, but I absolutely assure you that it is not. I played all of that, and it was one of the most fun tables I ever played and I dearly miss it, despite my character being a &quot;vegan&quot; vampiric monster with thousands of years with humanity 10 and a &quot;Buddha-like&quot; annoying new age speech - in fact, I took inspiration in the historical real life Buddha to play him, as I began to walk the path towards Golconda, and it was quite a transformation from wanna be tyrant to &quot;enlightened supreme being&quot;.<br /> <br /> That was a fun table. You don't need to believe me, I don't really care, I have the memories of that amazing game and it will follow me forever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T19:56:49-08:00","id":1546949,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1545147#post1545147\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> One Gehenna game I ran had the Giovanni pull off their plan to bring down the barrier between the mortal realm and the shadowlands. But there was a hitch, rather than merge the two realms, it just made the relatively fresh dead rise as zombies all across the world. I ran the zombies like the World War Z types which moved relatively quickly and thus the world collapsed. But here is the twist, the zombies don't react to vampires unless attacked. So now the vampire community is stuck in an interesting position where they have to gather up and protect the surviving humans just so they have a food supply in these final nights..</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow, that's an awesome setting! Turning the vamps from predator monsters to... Well, shepherds saviors... And still predator monsters, but it's either &quot;serve the blood masters&quot; or die at the hands of the &quot;walking dead&quot;.<br /> <br /> But I really liked that one","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-14T06:39:54-08:00","id":1546964,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1546917#post1546917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not quite sure you do understand completely - the lore changes you described, such as eleminating the Giovanni and Thoth's two cities don't appear to have any effect on the tropes themselves. Vampires still get destroyed by the sun, it's still a grimdark setting, angsty teen vamps are still dressing in trenchcoats and bemoaning their fate, etc.<br /> <br /> Magus Maximus' Second City game, as described, does appear to be one that breaks the tropes and stomps on the pieces though. Which, while I wouldn't be interested in that sort of a game, <i>doesn't make it any less valid</i>.<br /> <br /> As you point out, as long as the players and the ST are enjoying the story, and the trope breaking is part of it, then it works for that table.<br /> <br /> It's hard for most people to neatly delineate the lines between theme and setting. John wick, for example, is a quintessential WoD vampire story, except that there are no vampires in it, only criminals. Every element would fit so neatly into the VtM setting that it's astonishing. A similar issue applies to Furies, the French TV series that has a similar setting to John Wick with crime families running Paris. With a few tiny tweaks you could drop the whole thing into WoD. Both of these have themes that perfectly match VtM but settings that are a step away.<br /> <br /> I think I've started rambling, so I'll stop now.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh I see, sorry I hadn't understood clearly (English is not even my third language and I've been terribly busy lately, anyway sorry) now I think I understand you better. Yes, the tropes wouldn't be affected by the elimination of a clan or another (or perhaps even 7 clans lol), but the feel, that is the theme of the game is a more delicate thing. It's indeed very hard to delineate these invisible lines clearly, but I think I see your point much better now. And now that I think about it, that's probably the reason why the story described by Magus Maximus made me react in disgust, because it clearly cross those said lines and the game is not Vampire anymore, it's something else entirely. <b>Still, of course it's not any less valid because of that, it's just not my cup of tea, so to speak.</b><br /> <br /> In any case, thank you very much for your input.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","user":"Herr Meister","date":". 12-14-2024, 11:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-14T09:07:16-08:00","id":1546967,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544582-chronicle-of-ages?p=1546949#post1546949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Wow, that's an awesome setting! Turning the vamps from predator monsters to... Well, shepherds saviors... And still predator monsters, but it's either &quot;serve the blood masters&quot; or die at the hands of the &quot;walking dead&quot;.<br /> <br /> But I really liked that one</div> </div> </div> </div>It got really interesting because the PCs had to decide what sort of colony style they would set up, what sort of new rules they would run, and how they would handle the daylight hours.<br /> <br /> This resulted in things like a colony which was a blood cult that saw vampirism as an ascended being, thus the mortals tried to emulate them by bunkering down during the day and only venturing out at night. Their main food supply was fishing so they didn't have to worry about farming or other day time activities.<br /> <br /> A different colony tried a neo-carthage approach to things with a council running things with the vampire being a member of said council. Specifically, the vampire was in charge of the defenses since they would augment the main colony fighters as ghouls. This resulted in a colony that functioned around the clock, but it had the usual issues of any city simulation game.<br /> <br /> Another player tried to hide their vampiric nature completely and just be one of the people who just happened to save the day, at night, on many occasions.<br /> <br /> Needless to say, as an ST I had a lot of fun causing absolute havoc with the players idea of utopia for a vampire/human colony.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}